A||||4||fierospeeder||Oil Pan Leak||||13
Z||000000||fierospeeder||10-20-1999||11:39 PM||||This is on my 2.5L 84 Fiero.  When i bought the car about 3 years ago it had a minor oil leak.  I decided to do it myself without an engine lift which wasn't good of an idea, but after i started the car the oil just pored out.  I told my friend, he fixes cars a lot, to help me out which he did.  He made a bar to go ontop of the engine to hold it with threaded hooks.  I bought some gaskets from pep boys it came with two cork gaskets for the side and two rubber ones for the timing cover, and the rear.  We ran the car and it didn't leak, but after a few months it started getting moist, eventually leaking about half a quart of oil.  I havn't drove my car for atleast a year, instead i drive my 87 tbird turbo.  Tomorrow, im going to buy an engine lift and planning on to fix everything on my car so i can drive in the winter.  Better traction then a tbird.  Anyhow, my question is, should i use any gaskets at all, or just rtv the whole thing like haynes says,because each time i did the job, it leaked from the front and back of the oil pan, the sides were fine.  I know the later fieros had one piece gasket sets.<BR>Thanks for the advice and i know the engine lift will be easier for me to do this.<BR>||||reg||13
Z||000001||deceler8||10-21-1999||08:34 PM||||While it wasn't on a Fiero, I did recently take the oil pan off my '51 Chevy truck 6 cylinder because it was leaking a little. I used nothing but RTV, and it does not leak.<P>RTV is very forgiving and doesn't require exact torquing of bolts to achieve a leak free seal. About all you have to be careful is to not use either too much or too little, though too much is better than not enough. Just be sure to let it cure for 24 hours before you fill up with oil and drive just in case you did use a little bit too much.<P>RTV is also easier than a gasket when you're dealing with limited space as you don't have to worry about stuff shifting during the install.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||10-22-1999||01:04 AM||||I'll bet your pan is warped, or more likely, the area around some of the bolt holes has pulled down. If so, you'll need to straighten the pan surface. Lay it on a flat surface, like a glass window and look for gaps.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Dan||10-24-1999||10:01 AM|||| Check out the thread on valve cover gaskets.<BR>Same thing, just make sure your surfaces are clean.You may also check for excessive blowby<BR>and if necessary do a compression check.What kind of oil pressure are you running?||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||10-24-1999||06:33 PM||||I had a grand Am with that same engine in it. When I rebuilt the engine I warped the oil pan by resting the engine on the pan just before I put it back in the car. It leaked (poured) oil out of the front and rear of the oil pan.  I tried  everything to get that thing to seal properly and the only thing that worked was to do what deceler did and use a lot of RTV silicone sealent. I only used a lot on the front and rear and applied the normal amount to the sides with no gaskets. make sure that the oil pan is absolutely oil free and clean before you do this or you will be doing it again. Its also a good idea to torque the bolts to spec so that non of them will back out on you, if you over tighten them you could distort the flange. A new oil pan would also work but thats expensive.   ||||reg||1
